\slide{
\cowel_html_element("h1"){\tcode{$identifiers}\br
\span(id="docnum"){P4234R2}}
}

\slide{
\slideh{Introduction}

P4234 proposes to conditionally or unconditionally allow the use of the dollar sign (\tcode{$}) in identifiers in C++.
A version of this proposal for C is planned.

\ul{
    \li{$identifiers is one of the most widely supported extensions to C and C++ and is supported by all major compilers}
    \li{It has been supported for decades and predates standardization of both C and C++}
    \li{It is widely used in practice and required by some tooling, e.g. for linker-defined symbols}
    \li{In C++26 we accidentally made this extension non-conforming}
}
}

\slide{
\slideh{Motivation \'EM DASH' Linker defined symbols}

One such example is armlink from the \ref("https://developer.arm.com/documentation/dui0803/f/Accessing-and-Managing-Symbols-with-armlink/Linker-defined-symbols"){ARM MDK toolchain}:

\Bquote{
Symbols that contain the character sequence \tcode{$$}, and all other external names containing the sequence \tcode{$$}, are names reserved by ARM.
}

\codeblock("cpp"){
// oops, none of these are valid identifiers in conforming C++ impls
extern int Image$$ER_ZI$$Base;
extern int Image$$ER_ZI$$Limit;
extern int Image$$ER_RO$$Base;
extern int Image$$ER_RO$$Limit;
extern int Image$$ER_RW$$Base;
extern int Image$$ER_RW$$Limit;
}
}

\slide{
\slideh{Motivation \'EM DASH' Linker defined symbols}
Workarounds are possible with some implementations, but are error prone and inherently non-portable

\br\br

For example GCC and Clang allow overriding the symbol name with \tcode{asm}:
\codeblock("cpp"){
extern int Image_ER_ZI_Base  asm("Image$$ER_ZI$$Base");
extern int Image_ER_ZI_Limit asm("Image$$ER_ZI$$Limit");
extern int Image_ER_RO_Base  asm("Image$$ER_RO$$Base");
extern int Image_ER_RO_Limit asm("Image$$ER_RW$$Limit");
extern int Image_ER_RW_Base  asm("Image$$ER_RW$$Base");
extern int Image_ER_RW_Limit asm("Image$$ER_RW$$Limit");
}
}

\slide{
\slideh{Motivation \'EM DASH' Symbol patching}
Some tooling supports patching existing definitions. This is commonly used for startup hooks.

\br

For example, armlink supports the \tcode{$Sub$$} and \tcode{$Super$$} prefixes to patch existing definitions.

\br

\codeblock("cpp"){
#   define WRAPPER_FUNC(__FUNC)     $Sub$$##__FUNC
#   define REAL_FUNC(__FUNC)        $Super$$## __FUNC
}

Convenience macros for this appear in RP2040 MDK headers. 
While this is currently conditionally fine in C, it is not conforming in C++26
}

\slide{
\slideh{Motivation \'EM DASH' Identifier namespacing}
Despite the myriad of available characters for identifiers, people do like using what their keyboards offer.
The dollar sign is one of those characters.

\codeblock("cpp"){
#define $inline_never      [[gnu::noinline]]
#define $inline($$option)  $inline_##$$option
#define $template          template for (auto _ : "")
$inline(never) int bar(int, char) {
    $template {
        constexpr auto ctx = std::meta::current_function();
        constexpr auto [... Idx] = 
            std::make_index_sequence<parameters_of(ctx).size()>{};
        return ([:variable_of(parameters_of(ctx)[Idx]):] + ... + 0);
    }
}
}
}



\slide{
\slideh{Motivation \'EM DASH' Stricter domains}
For example, NATO IFB-CO-13859-TRITON states:
\Bquote{
    5.5.6 TRITON software will be developed with any variations from the languages or specifications given below as "Preferred Languages":

    C++ [ISO/IEC 14882]


    [T1-R1791]
    TRITON shall comply with the standards and language specifications given in the Description as the Preferred Languages. 
    \b{Any variations from the languages or specifications shall be agreed with the Purchaser}
}
}

\slide{
\slideh{Allowing unconditionally \'EM DASH' Teachability}

Leaving \tcode{$identifiers} conditionally supported (either by actually saying so or by omission) is less than ideal for teaching and learning purposes.

\codeblock("cpp"){
#define foo$bar 3
int main(){
    return foo$bar;
}
}
Is this a valid program?

\br

This question is not possible to answer without knowing details of the implementation.
Experience shows people assume this is fine, even though it is currently non-portable.
}

\slide{
\slideh{Allowing unconditionally \'EM DASH' Consistency}
\codeblock("cpp"){
#define f(x) #x
#define g(x) f(x)
#define foo bar
g(foo$)
}

With conditional support for \tcode{$identifiers}, this program expands to either one of
\ul{
    \li{\tcode{"bar$"} if dollar identifiers are \b{not} supported}
    \li{\tcode{"foo$"} if dollar identifiers are supported}
} 

This is a silent change in behavior and can lead to subtle bugs
}

\slide{
\slideh{Allowing unconditionally \'EM DASH' Impl Reality}

\table{
\tr{
    \th{Compiler}
    \th{Default}
    \th{CLI option}
}
\tr{
    \td{Clang}
    \td{✅ allowed}
    \td{\tcode{-f[no-]dollars-in-identifiers}}
}
\tr{
    \td{GCC}
    \td{✅ allowed}
    \td{\tcode{-f[no-]dollars-in-identifiers}}
}
\tr{
    \td{MSVC}
    \td{✅ allowed}
    \td{\tcode{/Za} (disables all MS extensions)}
}
\tr{
    \td{nvc++}
    \td{✅ allowed}
    \td{\nowrap{\tcode{-optk_allow_dollar_in_id_chars}}}
}
\tr{
    \td{icx}
    \td{✅ allowed}
    \td{\tcode{-f[no-]dollars-in-identifiers}}
}
\tr{
    \td{AOCC}
    \td{✅ allowed}
    \td{\tcode{-f[no-]dollars-in-identifiers}}
}
\tr{
    \td{\cowel_html_element("span", (class="nowrap")){Fujitsu BS2000 C/C++}}
    \td{✅ allowed}
    \td{\tcode{-K [no_]dollar}}
}
\tr{
    \td{EDG}
    \td{\nowrap{❌ not allowed}}
    \td{\tcode{--dollar}}
}
\tr{
    \td{TASKING TriCore}
    \td{\nowrap{❌ not allowed}}
    \td{\tcode{--dollar}}
}
}
}

